    mechanics: 'A dependency scoreboard marks each warp’s pending results; a warp is eligible when its next instruction’s inputs are ready and its target pipe can accept it. Among eligible warps the scheduler applies a simple policy (commonly loose round-robin with age preference, undocumented in detail), issuing one instruction per cycle; with 4 schedulers an SM issues at most 4 warp-instructions per cycle. Fixed-latency arithmetic (FP32 FMA ~4 cycles on recent generations, example) can be covered by 4 independent instructions or 4 eligible warps; variable-latency loads from L2 or HBM (hundreds of cycles) need tens of warps or deep software pipelining, which is why memory-bound kernels care about occupancy and compute-bound ones can run at 25 percent occupancy with enough ILP. `ncu` reports the warp state sampling: `stall_long_scoreboard` (waiting on global memory), `stall_short_scoreboard` (shared memory or special function unit), `stall_barrier`, `stall_mio_throttle` (memory instruction queue full), `stall_wait` (fixed-latency dependency), `selected` (issued) and `not_selected` (eligible but another warp won). `issue-slot utilization` below ~50 percent with few eligible warps per scheduler means latency is not hidden. Since Volta each thread has its own program counter (independent thread scheduling), so divergent branches interleave rather than serialize strictly, but a divergent warp still issues each path separately and pays the full issue cost for masked lanes. Tensor-core instructions on Hopper (`wgmma`) are issued asynchronously by a warpgroup of 4 warps and complete in the background, letting the scheduler keep issuing loads for the next tile; this is the mechanism warp-specialized kernels (producer warps doing TMA loads, consumer warps doing MMA) rely on. There is no preemption within a warp; a kernel that spins on a flag set by another block can deadlock if that block is never resident.',
