    subtitle: 'The device-wide cache between all SMs and HBM: 50 MB on H100 (example), physically split in two partitions.',
    subsystem: 'gpu',
    modes: ['system', 'memory', 'forward', 'kernelmode'],
    aliases: ['last-level cache', 'LTC', 'L2 slice', 'persisting L2', 'access policy window'],
    keywords: ['50 MB', 'L2 partition', 'crossbar', 'cudaAccessPolicyWindow', 'cudaLimitPersistingL2CacheSize', 'hit rate', 'lts__t_sectors', 'atomics', 'compression', 'MIG L2 slice'],
    summary: 'The L2 is the point of coherence for the GPU: every global load, store and atomic from any SM goes through it, and it is the last on-chip stop before HBM. On H100 SXM it is 50 MB (example) with aggregate bandwidth several times that of HBM, split into two partitions connected by a crossbar so that an SM reading data homed in the far partition sees higher latency. Its size is large enough to hold a decode step’s KV blocks for a few sequences or a small model’s activations, but far too small for weights, so its main effect on LLM kernels is reuse across thread blocks within one kernel.',
    purpose: 'Absorb reuse across SMs, coalesce writes, serve atomics, and reduce HBM traffic so that the memory system runs closer to its peak.',
    mechanics: 'The L2 is sector-based (32-byte sectors within 128-byte lines) and write-back; SMs access it through the crossbar at ~200-300 cycles latency (example, far more than L1). Each memory controller owns a set of L2 slices and physical addresses are hashed across slices, so bandwidth is spread but the two partitions on H100 mean roughly half of all accesses cross the partition link; this is what makes some grids show ~5-10 percent variance depending on which SMs get which blocks. Global atomics execute in the L2, which is why device-wide reductions and counters scale with L2 atomic throughput, not with SM count. Since Ampere a fraction of the L2 can be set aside for persisting accesses (`cudaDeviceSetLimit(cudaLimitPersistingL2CacheSize, bytes)` plus a stream `cudaAccessPolicyWindow`) so that a hot region such as a KV block table, a small embedding table or a router matrix stays resident while streaming traffic is marked evict-first. In a weight-streaming decode step each weight byte is read once per forward and gets no L2 reuse, but the activation vector and K/V for the current sequences fit and hit; in a GEMM, blocks that share A rows or B columns and run at the same time hit in L2 (block scheduling order and swizzled block-to-tile mapping are tuned for this). MIG partitions the L2 along with the SMs, so a 1g instance gets a proportional slice. `ncu` reports L2 hit rate, sectors read from L2 vs from device memory, and L2 throughput; a kernel at ~90 percent of HBM bandwidth with a low L2 hit rate is at the memory roofline, one with high L2 throughput and low HBM use is L2-bound.',
    input: 'Sector requests from all SMs (loads, stores, atomics, TMA) and copy engines.',
    output: 'Hits served from on-chip SRAM; misses forwarded to the memory controllers; write-back to HBM on eviction.',
    tensorShape: 'H100 SXM (example): 50 MB, 2 partitions, 128-byte lines with 32-byte sectors.',
    memory: 'Capacity example: 50 MB holds about 25 M BF16 values, roughly the KV of a few thousand tokens for a mid-size model, or one 128 × 8192 × 8192 BF16 GEMM operand slice; never a layer’s weights of a 70 B model.',
    compute: 'L2 bandwidth (several TB/s on H100, example) sets the ceiling for kernels whose working set fits; atomics throughput bounds device-wide reductions.',
    knobs: ['Persisting L2 window size and access policy per stream', 'Block-to-tile mapping (rasterization / swizzle) in GEMM kernels', 'Grid ordering so concurrently running blocks share operands', 'Data layout that keeps hot metadata compact', 'Cache hints in loads (`ld.global.cg`, evict-first/last)'],
    metrics: ['L2 hit rate and sectors from device memory (`ncu`)', 'L2 throughput vs HBM throughput per kernel', 'Atomic throughput at the L2', 'Partition imbalance (per-slice counters)'],
    failureModes: ['A working set slightly larger than L2 that thrashes and drops to HBM speed', 'Global atomics as the reduction strategy for large all-reduce-like kernels', 'Expecting weights to be cached across decode steps', 'Persisting window set larger than the configured limit and ignored', 'Block ordering that spreads operand reuse over time instead of concurrently'],
    trainingVsInference: 'Training GEMMs are sized so that operand reuse across concurrent blocks makes the L2 hit rate high. Inference decode is dominated by weight streams with no reuse; the L2 mainly helps with KV metadata, small tensors and the batched activation vector.',
    related: ['hbm', 'shared-memory-l1', 'streaming-multiprocessor', 'gpu-die', 'memory-coalescing', 'gemm-kernel', 'mig-mps-timeslicing', 'roofline'],
    references: ['hopper-whitepaper', 'cuda-guide', 'hopper-tuning', 'nsight-compute'],
