    summary: 'Data-center GPUs protect HBM with ECC (enabled by default on H100, example) that corrects single-bit errors and detects double-bit errors, and remap failing rows to spare rows so a card stays usable. Faults that ECC cannot hide, or that occur elsewhere (NVLink, PCIe, SM logic, the driver), are reported by the kernel driver as Xid events in `dmesg`. At the scale of thousands of GPUs, some fail every day, and a fraction of failures are silent, which sets the checkpoint interval and the validation strategy of long training runs.',
    purpose: 'Detect, contain and report hardware faults so that a job fails fast and observably rather than producing wrong numbers.',
    mechanics: 'HBM ECC on Hopper is on-die/side-band and on by default with no capacity or bandwidth cost visible to the application (example; earlier GDDR parts reserved capacity); correctable errors are counted (`nvidia-smi -q -d ECC`, volatile and aggregate) and uncorrectable ones terminate the affected context (Xid 48 double-bit error, or Xid 94 contained / Xid 95 uncontained ECC errors on Ampere and later, where a contained error kills only the affected process). Row remapping (Ampere onward, replacing page retirement) records a failing row and, after a reboot or reset, swaps in a spare; `nvidia-smi -q -d ROW_REMAPPER` shows pending and failed remaps, and Xid 63/64 mark a remap recorded or a remap failure, the latter being a replace-the-card signal. Other Xids of note: 13 (illegal instruction or graphics engine exception, often a kernel bug), 31 (GPU MMU fault, an out-of-bounds access), 43 (a process hit a GPU exception, reset the context), 45 (preemptive cleanup after a context was torn down), 74 (NVLink error), 79 (GPU has fallen off the bus, usually PCIe or power, requiring a reboot), 119/120 (GSP firmware RPC timeouts). A GPU that reports Xid 79 disappears from `nvidia-smi` and every CUDA call in the process returns an error; NCCL in the other ranks blocks until a timeout (`NCCL_TIMEOUT`, minutes by default) and the whole synchronous job restarts from the last checkpoint. Silent data corruption, where a compute unit produces a wrong result without any error, is rarer but real at fleet scale; defences are periodic `dcgmi diag -r 3/4` runs, redundant computation on suspect nodes, loss and gradient-norm anomaly detection, NaN/Inf watchdogs and comparing checksums of replicated tensors across data-parallel ranks. Checkpoint cost (`bytes ÷ storage bandwidth`) versus expected loss (`time since last checkpoint × failure rate`) gives the interval; large runs checkpoint every tens of minutes to a few hours and stage asynchronously through host memory. `dcgmi health -c` and DCGM fields (`XID_ERRORS`, `ECC_*`, `NVLINK_*`, `PCIE_REPLAY_COUNTER`) feed the alerting that drains a node before it wastes a job.',
